  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\n\nThe strategy involves representing the step function h as a sequence of non-overlapping binary \"1-blocks\" strategically placed in [0,2]. The central idea is to distribute these 1-blocks such that they are separated enough to minimize maximum overlap with their shifted versions. This approach leverages the insight that significant overlap occurs only when shifts align performance blocks. By exploring candidate placements at different mass distributions (binary or fractional) and inter-block spacings, we aim to systematically move away from abrupt transitions and reduce heavy overlaps at common shift amounts. We use a hybrid method combining gradient-based refinement with binary evaluations to explore configurations efficiently.\n\nThe parent solution (95 points) is used to initialize the search, but candidates are generated at any resolution via interpolation. The bottleneck addressed is the need to avoid long execution times for exact overlap calculations\u2014which are O(n_points^2)\u2014while ensuring systematic escape from local optima.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n\nVariables:\n- n_points: Number of sample points, dx=2.0/n_points\n- h: vector of n_points elements, each 0 or fractional, satisfying 0\u2264h[i]\u22641 and sum(h)=n_points/2\n\nObjective: \nFor any h, compute:\n    overlap(i) = np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full')\n    object = max(overlap(i)) * dx  (dx=2.0/n_points)\n\nRepresentation:\n- In binary mode: h[i] \u2208 {0,1} with exactly sum(h)=n_points/2\n- In fractional mode: h[i] \u2208 [0,1] with sum=h[i] = n_points/2\n\nConstraint: 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1, sum(h) = n_points/2\n\nDerivation:\nThe prove is based on the observation that significant overlap is minimized when binary transitions are separated by appropriate gaps. This requires careful testing of candidate configurations rather than theoretical proofs.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n\nSteps:\n\n1. Initialization:\n   - Input: initial_h_values (95-point array)\n   - Allow n_points to be 95 (given parent) or higher\n   - Represent h in two formats: binary and real (for finer control)\n   - Compilation:\n        * Read initial_h_values\n        * Handle interpolation between different n_points using np.interp\n\n2. Objective function for h:\n   - Function: compute_objective(h, n_points)\n        Requires: 0\u2264h[i]\u22641, sum(h)=n_points/2\n        returns: object_value = max(np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full')) * (2.0/n_points)\n\n3. Repair function:\n   - Function: repair_h(h, n_points)\n        Input: any h in the possible space\n        Output: 0\u2264new_h[i]\u22641, sum(new_h)=n_points/2\n        Steps:\n            * Scale current h to sum to n_points/2\n            * Clamp values to [0,1]; adjust subsequently if necessary\n\n4. Crossover functions:\n   - One-point crossover for binary:\n        Child: one half comes from parent1, the other from parent2\n        Repair after crossover (ensure sum=0.5*n_points)\n\n   - Real crossover:\n        Blend two real vectors\n\n5. Gradient-based refinement:\n   - Use finite differences to approximate gradients\n   - BFGS algorithm with repair mechanism\n   - Repair decision: every N steps or when violating boundary\n\n6. Optimization loop:\n\n   Constants:\n   - MAX_ITERATIONS = 100\n   - MAX_TIME = 1300 (seconds)\n\n   nnstructions:\n   * Initialize population using initial_h_values, upsampled to requested n_points\n   * For each generation:\n        - Evaluate fitness of all parents\n        - Perform binary crossover and repair\n        - Perform real-valued crossover/repair\n        - Perform local gradient-based refinement (BCGO-like algorithm)\n\n   * Terminate when time exceeds MAX_TIME\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n\nBinary valuation: considered to explore binary step functions, which may yield better overlap performance.\n\nFractional valuation: can lead to better overlap by allowing gradual transitions.\n\n   Densities explored: start at initial_h_value's points (95) then jitter (2-5% change), then binary/real crossover.\n\n   Repair mechanism: during initialization and after mutations, always repair to valid configuration.\n\n   Crossover: one-point binary crossover (with repair), real crossover: blend factor 0.5\n\n   Mutation: Gaussian perturbation with repair\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n\nDominant Time:\n- Objective calculation: O(n_points^2)\n- Population size and generations: typically 10x n_points, but adaptive M:N ratio\n\nFalling back:\n- End with the initial_h_values interpolation if time expires.\n- Always repair intermediate results\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n\n   After optimization:\n   - Ensure 0<=h[i]<=1, and sum(h)==n_points/2 (float equality 0.5*n_points)\n\n   Return:\n   - h: the best solution found (list of floats)\n   - c5_bound: the calculated objective from the bestCandidate\n   - n_points: the resolution used\n\n   Validation function:\n   - Automatically call compute_objective(h, n_points)\n   - Ensure finite result\n\n   Checks:\n   - No NaN or inf in gradient/h\n   - n_points as parameter\n\n   Objective consistency: will recompute via np.correlate\n\n7. Variable stopping conditions\n\n   Termination if:\n   - Time exceeded\n   - Convergence ( objective/min_diff_threshold reached)\n\n   Note: real crossover and binary crossover are used in combination. The best solution is remembered.",
